Let the suspend check go back to a CALL, under an env gate.

Patch 0059 replaced the `_ecv_suspended` call after every guest call and syscall
with a read of the `__ecv_unwinding` WASM GLOBAL, because that read is one
`global.get` where the call was a call -- 33,540 sites on a linked bash module.
That is the right default and stays the default.

It is also the one thing that stops WASIX loading a raptormark side module.
Measured against wasmer 7.3.0 (see .agents/docs/JOURNAL.md, 2026-08-24):

    $ wasmer run --mapdir /libs:libs host.wat
    failed to load module: Expected import to be a function: 'env'.__ecv_unwinding

WASIX's dynamic linker requires every `env` import to be a FUNCTION. Our side
modules import four globals; wasmer supplies the three standard PIC ones
(`__stack_pointer`, `__memory_base`, `__table_base`) itself and rejects ours.
A library of the identical shape, with `_ecv_suspended` imported as a FUNCTION
instead, loads with rc=0 -- so this single import is the whole difference.

`ECV_SUSPEND_VIA_CALL=1` restores the pre-0059 call form. Nothing else changes:
`_ecv_suspended` is still defined (ecvisor's `runtime/src/intrinsics.rs` returns
`context::unwinding()`, the same value the global holds; elfconv's own
`runtime/VmIntrinsics.cpp` returns 0 for the same reason it did before 0059), so
the two forms are semantically identical and differ only in cost.

⚠️ READ ONCE into a static. This runs at every suspend-check site, and a
`getenv` per site would be measurable on a lift that emits tens of thousands.

⚠️ DECLARE THE FUNCTION IF IT IS ABSENT. After 0059 nothing else references
`_ecv_suspended`, so the module it is lifted into may no longer carry a
declaration, and `module->getFunction` would return null -- the pre-0059 code
could assume it because the call site itself was the reference. The parameter
type is taken from `runtime_ptr` rather than written out, so this cannot drift
from whatever the caller actually holds.

